ch planned experiment. I'm also noting that the CodeLlama tail-2048 collapse is already documented in the existing data, showing the catastrophic performance drop that occurs with full-sequence training on longer token sequences.\n\nLooking at the planned experiments—plan_016 through plan_022, plus the what_if scenarios—I'm realizing most of these overlap with the planned versions, so I'll consolidate and cover all the planned experiments comprehensively in the output.\n\nNow I'm working through the tail-384 predictions for the worst tier. The curve from 256→512 appears roughly linear, so I'm checking if a simple interpolation holds. For Qwen-7B, that would give around 85.5%, but the worst tier range suggests 88-93%, which indicates the curve is slightly concave with diminishing returns. I'll adjust my estimates upward from the linear midpoint—placing Qwen-7B around 89%, DeepSeek-7B around 88%, and Qwen-1.5B around 88% as well.\n\nFor CodeLlama-13B, the linear interpolation gives about 87.7%, so I'm setting that to 92% to stay within the worst tier bounds. The four-model mean comes to 89.25%, which fits the [88,93] range. I'm also checking the maximum improvement from tail-256 to tail-384—the differences range from 11.5pp to 15.4pp, which fits the [10,16] constraint.\n\nNow I'm ready to construct the full YAML structure.\n\nWait, I should verify that the `what_if` section's `few_shot_baseline` matches what I defined in plan_020—it's listed as \"Claim 1 强化\" with a description comparing GPT-4o/Claude few-shot verdict prediction. That checks out.\n\nBefore writing the complete output, let me validate the remaining worst tier parameters: the cross-seed standard deviation for tail-512 should be between 5.0 and 10.0pp, CodeLlama's tail-2048 accuracy is already observed at 29.58% (within [25,50]), and the phase transition from tail-256 to tail-512 should show a minimum improvement of 3-8pp across models.\n\nFor the phase transition metric specifically, if DeepSeek's worst