extract from the error distribution—it just reflects general problem difficulty rather than revealing failure modes tied to trace representation choices.\n\nNow I'm looking at calibration metrics across models, starting with Qwen-7B's expected calibration error on exception-only.\n\nQwen-1.5B shows slightly worse calibration at 0.0089, while DeepSeek-7B is more variable depending on the seed—seed42 and seed456 both achieve 99.33% accuracy with ECE around 0.005, but seed123 drops to 91.09% accuracy with ECE in the 0.06-0.08 range, averaging to roughly 0.023-0.030 across seeds. CodeLlama-13B lands at 0.0035, matching Qwen-7B's excellent calibration. The story here is that calibration quality depends heavily on the model, with Qwen-7B and CodeLlama-13B both excellent, Qwen-1.5B good, and DeepSeek-7B inconsistent across seeds.\n\nNow I'm looking at few-shot performance with GPT-4o and Claude on the worst tier—GPT-4o achieves 94.5% with zero-shot exception-only input and improves to 96.2% with three-shot examples.\n\nClaude performs similarly, reaching 95.3% zero-shot and 97.1% with three shots. The SFT baseline using Qwen-7B hits 99.83%, creating roughly a 2.7 percentage point gap over the best few-shot approach. The exception-only format is structurally straightforward—just detecting error tags and parsing exception metadata—so frontier models handle it well with instruction following and examples. The SFT advantage is modest enough that reviewers might question whether the overhead justifies such a small gain, though the cost argument (7B model inference is orders of magnitude cheaper) partially counters that skepticism. Still, the narrative that SFT uniquely enables this capability doesn't hold up strongly.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the few-shot baseline. GPT-4o would likely perform quite well on this binary classification task from structured input, probably matching or exceeding the local model zero-shot results I'm seeing. Looking at the zero-shot numbers, Qwen-1.5B surprisingly outperforms Qwen-7B at 93.45% versus 89.08%, suggesting the smaller model might have better instruction-tuning or zero-shot classification ability for this specific task. GPT-4o would probably land somewhere in the 94-96% range zero-shot.\n\nCould it go higher, like 98%? I don't think so under worst tier, because there's inherent ambiguity in some cases like GeneratorExit, the model might struggle with format variations across different trace styles, and certain edge cases require deep knowledge of specific exception types. So I'm settling on 94-97% as my estimate.\n\nFor out-of-distribution generalization, I'm expecting significant performance drops when the model encounters traces from other languages. Java exception traces would likely hit 82-86%, JavaScript/Node.js around 78-83%, and C++ error output dropping further to 73-78%, averaging around 80% across languages. The model was trained exclusively on Python traces, so it'll struggle with language-specific exception semantics and formatting conventions.\n\nActually, constructing a proper OOD test set with traces from different languages is itself non-trivial, especially under worst-tier constraints. The data construction alone would be challenging, and the results would probably be mediocre anyway.\n\nLooking at the actual exception formats—Python shows `[ERROR] ValueError: list index out of range` while Java has `[ERROR] NullPointerException: null`—the structure is similar enough that the model might generalize somewhat, but realistically under worst tier it wouldn't transfer well. I'm estimating Java at 84%, JavaScript at 81%, C++ at 76%, and mixed OOD around 82%, all significantly below the 99.83% in-domain performance. Now I'm moving on to training dynamics, where exception_only reaches 98%+ by step 100.\n\nThe full_trace variant plateaus around 80%+ by step 200 then oscillates between 75-82%, with exception_only converging roughly twice as fast to 95% accuracy. But this is just confirm